{"archetypes":[{"name":"token-completeness","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"Verifies the plan's file list is exhaustive: every live-surface file referencing the retired argv token must appear in the plan's scope, since omissions become stale artifacts post-merge.","prompt_body":"Grep for the retired argv token across all *.md, *.sh, *.json, *.txt, *.tsv, and *.toml files in the repo, excluding larch-logs/, .git/, and CHANGELOG.md, then compare every hit file against the plan's stated file list. Flag any file that contains the retired token but is not scheduled for editing in the plan. Check that the plan's own completeness-grep exclusion list is accurate — verify whether fixture files, tsv rows, or README examples exist outside the listed scope. Also confirm that every test file pinning the retired token is updated rather than left pointing at removed prose, and that no live skill, hook, or agent Markdown outside the listed files embeds the token as a flag example. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"outcome-retire-trace","focus_area":"architecture","weight":1,"rationale":"Traces the cancelled-tier-gate outcome from its sole claimed producer through every consumer to confirm the retirement is complete and no consumer is left expecting a now-dead outcome token.","prompt_body":"Identify every file that reads, emits, documents, or tests the cancelled-tier-gate outcome token, checking both the plan's listed files and any unlisted files that reference it. Confirm the plan's claim that the interactive tier gate is the sole producer by scanning SKILL.md, scripts, and hooks for any remaining code path that could still emit this outcome after the plan's edits. Verify that the SKILL.md SUMMARY_OUTCOME enum, render-final-summary.sh case branch, render-final-summary.md contract doc, test-render-final-summary.sh, and render-run-summary.md are all mutually consistent after retirement with no file listing the token while another removes it. Check whether any external consumers such as the audit-runs skill or run-log parsers lie outside the plan's scope but depend on parsing this outcome token from committed logs. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
